---
import CarpeItDoc from '../../layouts/CarpeItDoc.astro';
import { carpeitOperator, complaintsBookUrl } from '../../data/carpeitOperator';

// Keep in step with app_config.terms_version on the server: changing it asks
// every user to accept again.
const lastUpdated = '13 October 2026';
---

<CarpeItDoc
  title="CarpeIt — Terms of Service"
  description="The terms for renting and lending water sports gear on CarpeIt."
  heading="Terms of Service"
  lastUpdated={lastUpdated}
>
  <p class="doc-lead">
    These terms are the agreement between you and CarpeIt when you use the app to rent gear or
    lend it. They also set out what renters and owners agree with each other. Please read them;
    you accept them in the app before you start, and again whenever they change.
  </p>

  <h2>1. What CarpeIt is</h2>
  <p>
    CarpeIt is a marketplace operated by {carpeitOperator.name} (NIPC {carpeitOperator.nipc}),
    registered at {carpeitOperator.address} (“CarpeIt”). It lets riders, schools and
    shops (“owners”) offer water sports equipment for rent, and lets other people (“renters”)
    book it. CarpeIt provides the app, takes payments, and keeps the record of each rental.
    CarpeIt does not own, inspect or supply the gear, and is not a party to the rental itself:
    the rental is an agreement between the renter and the owner.
  </p>
  <p>
    Owners are either private individuals or businesses (schools and shops), and every listing
    says which. When you rent from a business, you have the rights EU and Portuguese consumer
    law gives you against a trader. When you rent from a private individual, that consumer law
    doesn't apply to the rental itself; these terms and general civil law still do.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Your account</h2>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li>You must be at least 18 and able to enter into a binding contract. You confirm this in the app before you start.</li>
    <li>Keep your sign-in secure. You are responsible for what happens on your account.</li>
    <li>Use accurate information, and one account per person. A school or shop can add a business profile to a personal account.</li>
    <li>You can delete your account at any time from Profile in the app.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>3. Listing gear</h2>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li>Only list gear you own or are authorised to rent out, that is safe to use, and that matches its description and photos.</li>
    <li>You set the daily price, the deposit, and when the gear is available. Keep your availability up to date.</li>
    <li>The pickup address you enter is shown only to renters whose booking you accept. The public listing shows an approximate area.</li>
    <li>CarpeIt may remove listings that break these terms or that people report as unsafe or misleading.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>4. Booking</h2>
  <p>
    A renter chooses dates and sends a booking request. Their card is authorised for the rental
    price when they request, but not charged. The owner then has 72 hours to accept or decline.
    If the owner accepts, the rental price is charged and the booking is confirmed. If the owner
    declines, or doesn't answer in time, the authorisation is released and nothing is charged.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each rental is for specific dates, so the 14-day right of withdrawal for distance contracts
    doesn't apply to it (Decree-Law 24/2014, which excludes leisure services for a specific
    date or period). You can cancel under section 8 instead.
  </p>

  <h2>5. Prices, fees and payouts</h2>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li>Renters pay the rental price shown before they book. CarpeIt doesn't add a fee on top for renters, and the price doesn't depend on the card used.</li>
    <li>CarpeIt charges owners a service fee, currently 8% of the rental price, taken from the price the renter pays. The fee and the amount the owner receives are shown before a listing is published and on every booking. CarpeIt issues owners an invoice for its fee.</li>
    <li>Owners are paid through Stripe. To accept bookings you must set up payouts with Stripe and accept <a href="https://stripe.com/legal/connect-account">Stripe's Connected Account Agreement</a>.</li>
    <li>You are responsible for any taxes due on what you earn.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>6. How listings are shown</h2>
  <p>
    Search shows active listings that match the sport, dates and area you choose, newest first.
    Nobody can pay to be shown higher, and CarpeIt doesn't rank its own interests above
    anyone's. Ratings show the average of verified reviews: only the renter and the owner of a
    completed rental can review each other, once, within 60 days of the rental ending.
    CarpeIt doesn't edit reviews, and removes them only when they break section 12.
  </p>

  <h2>7. Deposits</h2>
  <p>
    If a listing has a deposit, a hold for that amount is placed on the renter's card from about
    24 hours before pickup, and kept while the gear is out. It is not a charge. The hold is
    released when the owner confirms the gear came back as expected. If the gear can't be held
    against a deposit, pickup can't go ahead until the renter updates their card.
  </p>

  <h2>8. Cancellations</h2>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li><strong>Renters</strong> can cancel a request at any time for free. After the owner accepts, cancelling is free until 48 hours before pickup and the rental price is refunded in full; after that, it isn't refunded.</li>
    <li><strong>Owners</strong> can cancel an accepted booking, and the renter is always refunded in full. Owners who cancel repeatedly may lose access to lending.</li>
    <li>The exact refund is shown in the app before you confirm a cancellation.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>9. Pickup and return</h2>
  <p>
    Both people use the app to photograph the gear at pickup and at return, following the
    prompts for that kind of equipment. These photos are the shared record of the gear's
    condition. The rental starts when pickup is confirmed. The renter must return the gear on
    time, clean, and in the condition it was received in, apart from normal wear.
  </p>

  <h2>10. Damage and disputes</h2>
  <p>
    If gear comes back damaged, missing or late, the owner can open a claim in the app before
    confirming the return, saying what happened and how much of the deposit they ask for. The
    renter then has 48 hours to accept or dispute it. If the renter accepts, that amount is
    taken from the deposit hold and paid to the owner, and the rest is released. If the renter
    disputes the claim or doesn't answer in time, the hold stays in place while CarpeIt reviews
    the handover photos, messages and anything else both people provide. CarpeIt then decides
    how much of the deposit, if any, goes to the owner, never more than the owner asked for,
    releases the rest, and tells you both why. The deposit is the most CarpeIt will take through the app; either of you keeps any
    other rights you have against the other, including going to a dispute resolution body or a
    court (section 16).
  </p>

  <h2>11. Safety and your responsibility</h2>
  <p>
    Water sports are dangerous. You are responsible for judging whether gear, conditions and
    your own ability are safe before you use anything you rent, and for using it within your
    skill level and local rules; you confirm this in the app before each booking. Owners are
    responsible for the condition of what they lend. CarpeIt doesn't check gear, people or
    spots, and doesn't provide insurance.
  </p>

  <h2>12. How to behave</h2>
  <ul class="doc-list">
    <li>Be honest, respectful and on time. No harassment, hate, threats, spam or scams.</li>
    <li>Keep bookings and payments in the app. Arranging payment outside CarpeIt removes the protections of the deposit and the rental record.</li>
    <li>Reviews must describe a real rental, honestly.</li>
    <li>You can report a user, message or listing from the app, and block anyone you don't want to hear from. Once blocked, neither of you can message or book the other.</li>
    <li>CarpeIt reviews reports and may remove content or suspend accounts that break these rules.</li>
    <li>You don't need an account to report illegal content: email <a href="mailto:hey@davidferreira.dev?subject=CarpeIt%20report">hey@davidferreira.dev</a> with a link or description of it and why it's illegal.</li>
    <li>When CarpeIt removes your content or limits your account, it tells you what it did, why, and which rule or law it relied on. You can ask for the decision to be reviewed by replying to that message.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>13. Liability</h2>
  <p>
    CarpeIt provides the app as it is. To the extent the law allows, CarpeIt is not liable for
    the gear, for what renters and owners do, or for injury, loss or damage arising from a
    rental, and CarpeIt's total liability to you is limited to the fees CarpeIt received from you
    in the twelve months before the claim. Nothing in these terms limits liability that cannot
    be limited by law, or your rights as a consumer.
  </p>

  <h2>14. Ending your use</h2>
  <p>
    You can stop using CarpeIt and delete your account whenever you like, once you have no
    rental in progress. CarpeIt may suspend or close accounts that break these terms, after
    telling you why unless there is a safety or legal reason not to.
  </p>
  <p>
    For schools and shops lending as a business, CarpeIt gives at least 30 days' notice before
    closing a business account for good, except for repeated or serious breaches of these terms
    or where the law requires it. Business owners can raise any complaint about CarpeIt by
    email, and CarpeIt answers within 15 days.
  </p>

  <h2>15. Changes and law</h2>
  <p>
    If these terms change, the new version is published here and announced in the app at least
    15 days before it applies, unless a law or a safety issue requires a change sooner. You'll
    be asked to accept the new version in the app; if you don't want to, you can close your
    account. These terms are governed by Portuguese law. If you are a consumer, you also keep
    the protection of the mandatory laws of the country where you live, and can bring a claim
    there.
  </p>

  <h2>16. Complaints and dispute resolution</h2>
  <p>
    You can complain to CarpeIt by email, or in the
    <a href={complaintsBookUrl}>electronic complaints book (Livro de Reclamações)</a>.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you're a consumer and we can't resolve a dispute, you can take it to a consumer
    arbitration centre (alternative dispute resolution entity). CarpeIt's is
    <a href="https://www.cniacc.pt">CNIACC – Centro Nacional de Informação e Arbitragem de Conflitos de Consumo</a>.
    The full list of approved entities is on the <a href="https://www.consumidor.gov.pt">Portal do Consumidor</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>17. Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions about these terms? Email <a href="mailto:hey@davidferreira.dev?subject=CarpeIt%20terms">hey@davidferreira.dev</a>
    or write to {carpeitOperator.name}, {carpeitOperator.address}.
    For how your data is handled, see the <a href="/carpeit/privacy/">Privacy Policy</a>.
  </p>
</CarpeItDoc>
